8. If you remove the second glPopMatrix(), the scene will initially look fine, but after:

resizing the window, or
covering/uncovering it (forcing redraws),

you’ll see objects drifting, duplicating, or moving incorrectly over time or disappearing even.

Why?

Because:

Every call to display() does a glPushMatrix() without a matching glPopMatrix().
The modelview stack grows indefinitely.
More importantly, the current matrix is not restored, so transformations accumulate between frames.


9. Originally, gluLookAt is applied once in main, setting the base modelview matrix.

If you move it inside display() after a glPushMatrix(), then:

The viewing transformation becomes part of the pushed matrix state
It is not applied globally, but only within that pushed scope

So now:

You push the current matrix
Apply gluLookAt (camera transform)
Apply object transforms
Pop → camera transform disappears

This breaks the intended separation:

Camera transform should be the base (bottom) of the stack
Object transforms should be applied on top of it

10. What happens?

The scene changes dramatically:

The camera effect from gluLookAt disappears
Objects appear as if viewed from the origin
You may not even see the teapots properly (they could be clipped or too close)
Why?

glLoadIdentity() resets the current matrix to the identity matrix:

M = I

If you call it in display() before drawing:

You wipe out the viewing transformation set by gluLookAt in main
So all drawing happens in camera-at-origin space

Result:

No camera offset (you’re effectively inside or too close to the scene)
The perspective still exists, but the view transform is gone
